          "page": 2,
          "layout": "text",
          "text": "a seamless manner. The system also allows their family members back home, if any, to claim \nthe balance of foodgrains on same the ration card.3 \nAt present, ONORC is effectively implemented in all 36 States and Union Territories, \nmaking food security portable throughout the country, with Assam becoming the last state to \nhave it implemented.4 \n \n \nGenesis5 \nThe One Nation One Ration Card reform has its genesis in the vision that the computerisation \nof the Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) in the country must be taken to the next \nlevel in ensuring food security in the country. \nThis system enables migrant beneficiaries to seamlessly get their food grains from any Fair Price \nShop of their choice, in any part of the country, by using their same/existing ration card with \nbiometric authentication of identity on an ePoS device. \nThis initiative also provides leverage to States/UTs to serve many more beneficiaries, without \nactually incurring any additional burden. Furthermore, this mechanism can plug the \nleakage/diversion of un-lifted food grains in FPSs, as they can now be lifted by their rightful \nbeneficiaries through portability and get reconciled/ adjusted between States/UTs, thereby \nminimizing the chances of un-lifted foodgrains getting diverted. \n \n \n \n \n3 https://pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1819025 \n4 https://pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?PRID=1847386#:~:text=The%20Dep \nartment%20of%20Food%20and,pilot%20project%20in%20four%20states. \n5 https://transformingindia.mygov.in/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/ONORC-eng.pdf",
